"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][32]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M32-L1 – Nonlinear Dynamics: Equilibria, Local Linearisation and Phase Portrait<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine equilibria and local\nstability, interpret a phase space and justify the range of validity of\na linearisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinear state model<\/h2>\n<p>A linear model can describe oscillations, storage and coupling, but a\nsingle global linear approach cannot capture state-dependent\ncoefficients or saturation. Material properties in electrical or thermal\nsystems are often temperature-dependent. The state-space form is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=f(x,u),\\qquad y=h(x,u).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An equilibrium with constant input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_e,u_e)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiple equilibria are possible. The state is a minimal (or at least\nsufficient) set of information for prediction under a given input; the\nmeasured quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nneed not represent it completely. It is advisable to scale dimensional\nquantities before numerical analysis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Model example:<\/strong> Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a dimensionless deviation and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntime normalised by a reference time. The autonomous system<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dx}{d\\tau}=x-x^3=x(1-x^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>has stationary points −1, 0 and +1. The signs of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the four open intervals are +, −, +, −. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na state initialised exactly there remains there without excitation; a\nsmall deviation initially grows. Each of the two outer equilibria\nattracts the states on its own side. This model is a mathematical normal\nform, not a measured characteristic of a specific device.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_088_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalised cubic vector field and trajectories towards the stable equilibria −1 and +1.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the normalized cubic system, trajectories\napproach stable equilibria at −1 or +1 depending on initial condition;\nzero separates their regions of attraction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local linearisation<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta x=x-x_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta u=u-u_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first-order Taylor expansion gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\dot x=A\\delta x+B\\delta u+O(\\|(\\delta x,\\delta u)\\|^2),\\quad A=\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right|_e,\\quad B=\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial u}\\right|_e.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Similarly, calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\partial h/\\partial x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\partial h/\\partial u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the cubic system,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1-3x_e^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nat ±1,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat zero,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe linear response is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta x(\\tau)=\\delta x(0)e^{A\\tau}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNegative real parts of all eigenvalues guarantee local exponential\nstability for a smooth autonomous system; an eigenvalue with positive\nreal part indicates instability. Eigenvalues on the imaginary axis alone\ndo not settle the general nonlinear case\n(e.g. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=-x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Numerical check:<\/strong> At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_e=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta x=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe linear derivative is −0.4, whereas the original gives −0.528. The\nlinearisation error is 0.128 in the normalised derivative. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta x=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe error is only 0.001208. As the perturbation decreases, the\ndiscrepancy decreases approximately quadratically, not linearly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Phase plane for two states<\/h2>\n<p>For a pendulum, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the angle (rad) and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe angular velocity (rad/s). An idealised model with damping is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>ω<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi>g<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>u<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\theta=\\omega,\\quad \\dot\\omega=-\\frac{g}{l}\\sin\\theta-b\\omega+\\frac{u}{J},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis gravitational acceleration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe length,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe normalised damping coefficient (1/s),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe moment of inertia and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe torque (Nm). At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nequilibria occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k\\pi,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe matrix<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\-g\\cos\\theta_e/l&amp;-b\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>has only eigenvalues with negative real parts for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_e=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_e=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe determinant is negative; one eigenvalue is positive. In the phase\nportrait, trajectories appear as curves whose tangents are determined by\nthe state-space vector field. A field plot is not a time axis. The\nentire curve describes which states are reached in succession; direction\nand speed follow from the vector field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical investigation<\/h2>\n<p>To map the reachable states, begin integration from several points.\nUse different initial values in the immediate vicinity of unstable\nequilibria and at least two step sizes. An apparently crossing\ntrajectory may indicate excessively coarse integration or inconsistent\ninputs. Unique solutions of a smooth autonomous system do not cross in\nthe full state space. Projections of a higher-dimensional system,\nhowever, can cross. See <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L3<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine all equilibria of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=2x-x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,\\pm\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe derivatives are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2,-4,-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the linearisation report for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=-x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the origin? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit does not determine stability. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever, the sign always points towards the origin.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the pendulum eigenvalues for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g/l=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻¹ at the downward equilibrium. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1\\pm j\\sqrt3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻¹.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a controller around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_e=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot automatically suitable for reaching that point from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> A different equilibrium, a nonlinear field\nand possibly input constraints. A local design does not guarantee a\nglobal trajectory.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Russ Tedrake, MIT –\nUnderactuated Robotics, Nonlinear Planning and Control / Lyapunov\nAnalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 2: simple pendulum, nonlinear dynamics; table of\ncontents only. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M32-L2 – Stability Analysis with Lyapunov Functions and Basins of Attraction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Check a Lyapunov function,\nderive an invariant subset and distinguish local from global\nstability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability as a property of trajectories<\/h2>\n<p>An equilibrium that is stable in the sense of Lyapunov remains within\nany prescribed neighbourhood for sufficiently small initial deviations.\nAsymptotic stability additionally requires convergence to the\nequilibrium from a neighbourhood. Exponential stability bounds the\ndeviation by a decaying exponential function. These terms refer to a\ndefined model, inputs and time interval; a measured steady curve alone\ndoes not prove them.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the equilibrium at zero, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe continuously differentiable, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a neighbourhood. The derivative along the solution is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V(x)=\\nabla V(x)^\\mathsf{T}f(x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstability follows under these local conditions; strictly negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naway from the origin establishes asymptotic stability. Global statements\nrequire additional conditions, such as a globally positive definite,\nradially unbounded function with a globally negative derivative away\nfrom the origin. Radial unboundedness means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|x\\|\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the derivative is only semidefinite, LaSalle’s invariance principle\non a compact positively invariant set can establish convergence to the\nlargest invariant subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basin of attraction in the cubic model<\/h2>\n<p>For <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;section=S1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L1<\/a>,\nchoose<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)=\\tfrac14(x^2-1)^2,\\quad \\frac{dV}{dx}=x^3-x,\\quad \\dot V=-(x^3-x)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The function is not globally positive definite relative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsince it is also zero at −1. Restrict the analysis to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;c&lt;1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe connected component of the sublevel set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\le c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naround +1 lies entirely in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>c<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x^2-1|\\le2\\sqrt c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollow the bounds<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>c<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>c<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{1-2\\sqrt c}\\le x\\le\\sqrt{1+2\\sqrt c}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1/16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately 0.707 to 1.225. Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na solution cannot leave this interval. Within it, +1 is the only zero of\nthe derivative; therefore convergence to +1 follows. Here the exact\nbasin of attraction is the entire positive half-line, as follows from\nthe signs of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn general, a certified sublevel set is only an inner estimate, not an\nexact boundary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_089_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Double-well potential of the cubic system and negative time derivative between equilibria.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The chosen Lyapunov function has minima at stable\nequilibria; its time derivative shows the decrease along the\ntrajectories considered away from stationary points.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mechanical energy function<\/h2>\n<p>For the damped, unforced pendulum from L1, the normalised mechanical\nenergy is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>g<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\theta,\\omega)=\\tfrac12\\omega^2+\\frac{g}{l}(1-\\cos\\theta),\\qquad \\dot V=-b\\omega^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The quantity has units of s⁻² for dimensionless radians.\nMultiplication by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the physical energy of the idealised point-mass pendulum. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe derivative is only semidefinite. A state with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains there only if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas well. For a connected energy sublevel set around the downward\nequilibrium with an energy level below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2g/l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe upper equilibrium is excluded; LaSalle’s principle establishes\nconvergence to the downward equilibrium. This energy bound refers to the\nmodel without external torque. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan additional term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega u/J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nappears; the energy can increase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quadratic functions for linear systems<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=Ax<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a Hurwitz matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(all eigenvalues in the open left half-plane), for each symmetric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≻<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\succ0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere is a unique symmetric matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>≻<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\succ0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermined by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^\\mathsf{T}P+PA=-Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=x^\\mathsf{T}Px<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V=-x^\\mathsf{T}Qx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExample:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1/4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a nonlinear system with remainder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x^\\mathsf{T}Pr(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|r(x)\\|\\le L\\|x\\|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a neighbourhood,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V\\le-\\lambda_{\\min}(Q)\\|x\\|^2+2\\|P\\|L\\|x\\|^3.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Within a sufficiently small ball, the derivative remains strictly\nnegative away from the origin. A sublevel set contained in it provides\nan invariant local estimate. Merely sampling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnumerically does not prove an inequality between sample points.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=-3x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=x^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndetermine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nglobally asymptotically and exponentially stable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a globally positive definite interpretation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(x^2-1)^2/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naround +1 fail? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> There is another minimum at\n−1.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the pendulum, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g/l=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhich initial velocities lie strictly below the energy barrier?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0^2/2&lt;8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\omega_0|&lt;4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>V<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat a single point already imply a stationary state?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No; in the pendulum, the derivative is zero\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacceleration occurs immediately.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Russ Tedrake, MIT –\nUnderactuated Robotics, Nonlinear Planning and Control / Lyapunov\nAnalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 9: Lyapunov functions, invariance and regions of\nattraction; table of contents only. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M32-L3 – State and Disturbance Estimation: Recursive Filters and Model Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the discrete Kalman\nrecursion, distinguish process and measurement uncertainty and model a\nconstant disturbance as an additional state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the observer to stochastic estimation<\/h2>\n<p>A state observer combines a model with measurements. For the discrete\nlinear model<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=Ax_k+Bu_k+w_k,\\qquad y_k=Hx_k+v_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_k,v_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe zero-mean, temporally white, independent of each other and of the\ninitial estimation error, with covariances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q,R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes process uncertainty per step;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes measurement uncertainty per sample. These matrices have\nsquared units of the corresponding state and measurement quantities.\nActual model errors need not be white: in that case, the chosen\ncovariance is a model, not evidence of real statistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Kalman filter<\/h2>\n<p>The index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k|k-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the prediction based on measurements up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k|k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the corrected estimate. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the error covariance, the equations are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msubsup><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><\/msubsup><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x_{k|k-1}=A\\hat x_{k-1|k-1}+Bu_{k-1},\\qquad P^-=AP^+_{k-1}A^\\mathsf{T}+Q,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=HP^-H^\\mathsf{T}+R,\\quad K=P^-H^\\mathsf{T}S^{-1},\\quad \\hat x_{k|k}=\\hat x_{k|k-1}+K(y_k-H\\hat x_{k|k-1}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The innovation is the difference between the actual and predicted\nmeasurement. The Joseph form of the updated covariance is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^+=(I-KH)P^-(I-KH)^\\mathsf{T}+KRK^\\mathsf{T}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It better preserves positive semidefiniteness in finite-precision\narithmetic. Numerically, one should solve a linear system rather than\nexplicitly calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder a linear model with Gaussian initial and noise distributions, the\nfilter is the conditional mean estimator; without the Gaussian\nassumption, it is the optimal linear estimator under the second-moment\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scalar example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=0.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK²,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK²,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA prediction of 24.0 °C and a measurement of 24.5 °C give an estimate of\n24.4 °C, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.032<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^+=0.032<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK². A higher assumed measurement variance typically reduces the weight\nof the innovation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Disturbance as an additional state<\/h2>\n<p>The thermal example from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L6<\/a>\ncan be written in discrete form with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"><\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=T-20\\,\\mathrm{^\\circ C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the temperature difference in K and unknown thermal power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin kW:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=ax_k+b(u_k+d_k),\\qquad d_{k+1}=d_k+\\eta_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Letting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(x,d)^\\mathsf{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>a<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_z=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad B_z=\\begin{pmatrix}b\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H_z=(1\\quad0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The observability matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>a<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>b<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\a&amp;b\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas rank two if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the ideal model, a constant load can thus be reconstructed from the\ntemperature sequence. This does not mean that every real heat loss can\nbe uniquely distinguished from every sensor bias or incorrect model\nparameter. If further unknown states are added, observability must be\nchecked again.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_090_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic noisy temperature measurements, reconstructed temperature and an estimate of unknown thermal power.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Noisy synthetic temperature readings are used to\nrecursively estimate both temperature and unknown heat input;\nmeasurements and estimated states are shown separately.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The figure uses a synthetic example with a sampling interval of 30 s,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=e^{-0.05}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(1-a)/0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK/kW. Heating power is 0.5 kW; the unknown load jumps from 0 to −0.7 kW\nand later returns to zero. Independent normally distributed errors with\na standard deviation of 0.15 K are superimposed on the measurements\n(seed 2603). The selected filter covariances are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.0025<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.004<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\operatorname{diag}(0.0025,0.004)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0225<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.0225<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK²,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.25)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat z_0=(0,0)^\\mathsf{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe disturbance estimate follows with a delay: the random-walk model did\nnot anticipate exactly known step times.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinear estimators and validation<\/h2>\n<p>The extended Kalman filter (EKF) uses the nonlinear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor prediction and its local Jacobian at the current estimate for\ncovariance propagation. The measurement operator is also locally\nlinearised. The EKF is therefore an approximate method; in the presence\nof ambiguity, large uncertainty or strongly nonlinear measurements, it\nmay converge poorly. Alternative approaches include the unscented Kalman\nfilter and particle filters, with greater computational and modelling\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<p>Check the mean innovation, its autocorrelation, the normalised\ninnovation squared\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu^\\mathsf{T}S^{-1}\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, if independent reference states are available, the actual\nestimation error. A plausible covariance signal is not automatically a\ncalibrated error bar. Increasing process covariance may improve\nestimation quality for steps but worsen it during steady phases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=0.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK². <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.5; both sources of information have\nequal variance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens in the scalar observable case as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\to1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe estimate adopts the perfect measurement.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot observable when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It does not affect the measured temperature;\nthe rank falls to one.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a temperature sensor independent of the model be useful for\nvalidation? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Otherwise the same bias can shift\nboth the measurement and the apparent reference.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ee363.stanford.edu/lectures.html\">Stanford EE363 –\nLinear Dynamical Systems, Lecture Slides: LQR and Kalman Filter<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nEE363 lecture index 12, 21 and 22: estimation and Kalman filter; slides\nnot reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M32-L4 – Finite-Horizon Optimal Control with Input Constraints<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L1<\/a>;\nsupplementary University Mathematics <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L12<\/a>\naccording to the curriculum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a finite-horizon\nquadratic optimal control problem and distinguish the unconstrained and\nconstrained cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cost and horizon<\/h2>\n<p>In optimal control, objectives are represented by costs. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=Ax_k+Bu_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a zero reference state, horizon\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≽<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≻<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≽<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\succeq0,R\\succ0,Q_f\\succeq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>Q<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(x_k^\\mathsf{T}Qx_k+u_k^\\mathsf{T}Ru_k)+x_N^\\mathsf{T}Q_fx_N.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The cost can be made dimensionless by normalising the quantities to\ncharacteristic scales; otherwise, the weights carry the appropriate\ninverse squared units. For reference tracking, use deviations from a\nfeasible steady-state pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_r,u_r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_r=Ax_r+Bu_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlimits control activity but does not replace a physical actuator limit.\nA sum of squared power values is not automatically energy consumed; the\nlatter depends on the physically defined power and time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic programming and Riccati recursion<\/h2>\n<p>For the unconstrained case, assume the optimal cost-to-go function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_k(x)=x^\\mathsf{T}P_kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_N=Q_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_k(x)=\\min_u\\{x^\\mathsf{T}Qx+u^\\mathsf{T}Ru+(Ax+Bu)^\\mathsf{T}P_{k+1}(Ax+Bu)\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Differentiating with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand setting the derivative to zero gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_k=(R+B^\\mathsf{T}P_{k+1}B)^{-1}B^\\mathsf{T}P_{k+1}A,\\qquad u_k^*=-K_kx_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Substitution gives the backward recursion<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_k=Q+A^\\mathsf{T}P_{k+1}A-A^\\mathsf{T}P_{k+1}BK_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under sufficient conditions (including stabilisability and\nappropriate detectability), the infinite-horizon LQR converges to the\nstabilising solution of the algebraic Riccati equation. With a finite\nhorizon, the optimal gain is generally time-dependent; a short horizon\nwithout a suitable terminal weight may be disadvantageous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Input limits in the scalar case<\/h2>\n<p>Consider the normalised model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=x_0+u_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=(2+u)^2+u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the unconstrained optimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5\\le u\\le0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe cost increases from 2 to 2.5. This follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dJ/du=4+4u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand convexity. For multiple steps, simply clipping each unconstrained\ninput independently is not sufficient: the optimal sequence is coupled\nthrough the state dynamics.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_091_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Scalar quadratic objective with an unconstrained minimum and a shifted solution under an input constraint.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: An unconstrained quadratic objective has an interior\nminimum; an input constraint shifts the selected control input to the\nboundary of the feasible region.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From dynamics to a quadratic programme<\/h2>\n<p>Stack\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=(x_1^\\mathsf{T},\\dots,x_N^\\mathsf{T})^\\mathsf{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=(u_0^\\mathsf{T},\\dots,u_{N-1}^\\mathsf{T})^\\mathsf{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy repeated substitution,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋮<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋮<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋮<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo>⋱<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋮<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=Fx_0+GU,\\quad F=\\begin{pmatrix}A\\\\A^2\\\\\\vdots\\\\A^N\\end{pmatrix},\\quad G=\\begin{pmatrix}B&amp;0&amp;\\cdots&amp;0\\\\AB&amp;B&amp;\\cdots&amp;0\\\\\\vdots&amp;\\vdots&amp;\\ddots&amp;\\vdots\\\\A^{N-1}B&amp;A^{N-2}B&amp;\\cdots&amp;B\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With block weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the stacked states and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>R<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the inputs, the cost, apart from a constant term, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>G<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>R<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>G<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U^\\mathsf{T}(G^\\mathsf{T}\\bar QG+\\bar R)U+2x_0^\\mathsf{T}F^\\mathsf{T}\\bar QGU<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInput constraints and linear state bounds give linear inequalities in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>R<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≻<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar R\\succ0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe objective is strictly convex: if the feasible set is non-empty,\nthere is a unique optimum. For a convex quadratic programme,\nsatisfaction of the KKT conditions is sufficient for optimality. With\nbox constraints alone, the residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U-\\Pi_{[U_{min},U_{max}]}(U-\\alpha\\nabla J(U))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith any fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be used as a test. Exactly zero means stationary with respect to the\nfeasible set. A threshold-based numerical residual is not an exact\nproof.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the one-step example with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2+u)^2+3u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalculate the unconstrained optimum. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the scalar example? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ncontrol activity becomes more costly than the deviation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the state bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeasible given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2,u\\ge-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No; the smallest attainable state is\n1.5.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the purpose of a terminal weight? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nIt assigns a cost to the state at the end of the finite horizon and can\napproximate long-term costs. Stability does not automatically follow\nfrom an arbitrary terminal weight.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ee363.stanford.edu/lectures.html\">Stanford EE363 –\nLinear Dynamical Systems, Lecture Slides: LQR and Kalman Filter<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nEE363 lecture index 16: finite-horizon LQR; partial scope, slides not\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://sites.engineering.ucsb.edu/~jbraw/mpc/\">Rawlings/Mayne/Diehl\n– Model Predictive Control: Theory, Computation, and Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2nd\ned., 6th printing, §§1.2.5, 1.3.1–1.3.3, 2.2–2.3 (pp. 6, 11–18, 91–107);\nPDF contents pages xv–xvi visually checked; no full claim review. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M32-L5 – Model Predictive Control: Receding Horizon, Constraints and Limits of Robustness<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate the online recursion\nof MPC, distinguish feasibility from stability, and assess the handling\nof disturbances and computation time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Control through repeated optimisation<\/h2>\n<p>The optimal control sequence from L4 is initially just a plan. Model\nPredictive Control (MPC) closes the loop by recalculating the plan\nwhenever new information becomes available. At time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe model predicts states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+i|k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere the second index denotes the time at which the plan is generated.\nA sequence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontrol inputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{k|k},\\dots,u_{k+N-1|k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis calculated, but only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{k|k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis applied. The controller then obtains a new state or estimate and\nsolves the problem again.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/munder><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_U\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\\ell(x_{k+i|k},u_{k+i|k})+V_f(x_{k+N|k})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>subject to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒵<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k|k}=\\hat x_k,\\quad x_{k+i+1|k}=f(x_{k+i|k},u_{k+i|k},d_{k+i|k}),\\quad (x,u)\\in\\mathcal Z.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The disturbance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be predicted, assumed constant or estimated by an observer. A\npredicted input is not identical to the actual future input. A linear\nmodel and convex quadratic cost with linear constraints give a QP; a\nnonlinear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntypically gives a nonlinear optimisation problem, potentially with local\nminima. Even with a linear model, non-convex constraints can make the\nproblem non-convex.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_092_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Prediction horizons at two consecutive sampling instants; only the first input of each plan is applied.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At each new sample instant, the prediction horizon\nshifts and is solved again; only the first move of the current control\nplan is implemented.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hard and soft constraints<\/h2>\n<p>A maximum actuator current is a hard constraint that must not be\nexceeded in the optimisation. A comfort temperature can be formulated as\na soft constraint:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le x_{max}+s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith a penalty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda s^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe soft constraint can improve feasibility, but it does not guarantee\nthat the original bound is respected. A real safety shutdown is still\nrequired independently of the optimisation. A defined fallback\ncontroller is necessary for infeasible problems or numerical\nfailures.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example:<\/strong> With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=0.9x_k+0.2u_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le u\\le2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe requirement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}\\ge5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot be satisfied. The maximum attainable value is 4.9. A soft lower\nbound requires at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a physical lack of control authority, not a failure of the\nnumerical optimiser.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursive feasibility and stability<\/h2>\n<p>Finding a feasible sequence once says nothing about whether another\nfeasible sequence exists after an actual disturbance. In the nominal\ncase, a terminal invariant set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒳<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal X_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a locally feasible terminal controller allow construction of a\ncandidate at the next step by shifting the previous sequence and\nappending a terminal action. If the terminal cost function also\nsatisfies an appropriate decrease inequality, the optimal cost function\ncan serve as a Lyapunov function under further assumptions. Without such\nconditions, stability depends on factors including the horizon, weights,\nmodel accuracy and feasible state set. Successfully solving a QP\nnumerically is not by itself a guarantee of stability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model error and disturbance loads<\/h2>\n<p>MPC takes account of predicted dynamics, but only according to the\nmodel used. A constant unknown load can cause a steady-state error if\nthe target state is paired with an incorrect equilibrium input.\nOffset-free MPC methods, for example, incorporate a disturbance model as\nin <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L3&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L3<\/a>\nand calculate a new feasible steady-state target from the estimate. A PI\ncontroller with an integral state can also reject constant disturbances,\nprovided there is sufficient control authority. An unknown load cannot\nbe fully rejected before it occurs.<\/p>\n<p>Robust MPC uses, for example, uncertainty sets and tightened nominal\nconstraints (tube MPC) or minimax criteria. Stochastic MPC formulates\nprobabilistic constraints under model assumptions. Both require explicit\nassumptions about uncertainty; an arbitrary safety margin does not prove\nrobustness. Implementation also depends on the sampling interval:\nestimation, optimisation, checking and output must be completed before\nthe deadline. A good solution obtained after the deadline may be useless\nfor the real control loop. The following practical assignment\ninvestigates simulated discrete-time control only, not a real-time\nguarantee.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With a sampling interval of 30 s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhow long is the prediction? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 360 s; initially,\nonly the first value is applied for 30 s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a soft constraint a safety guarantee? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nNo; a violation is explicitly allowed and merely penalised.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does recursive feasibility mean? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nFrom an initially feasible situation, the next optimisation problem\nremains feasible under the specified conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two causes of steady-state MPC tracking error.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> An unmodelled constant disturbance or an\nincorrect steady-state model; an unattainable reference may also force a\npersistent error.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://sites.engineering.ucsb.edu/~jbraw/mpc/\">Rawlings/Mayne/Diehl\n– Model Predictive Control: Theory, Computation, and Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2nd\ned., 6th printing, §§2.2, 2.4–2.6 and 3.1–3.2 (pp. 91, 112–144,\n193–209); PDF contents pages xv–xvi visually checked; no full claim\nreview. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M32-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M32-L6 – Specialisation Project: Comparing Linear Control and MPC for a Thermal System under Disturbances<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master’s Specialisation: Nonlinear and Model Predictive Control<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L5<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M28-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reproduce a fairly documented\ncontroller comparison under specified model deviations and discuss its\nperformance and robustness limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental plan<\/h2>\n<p>A small idealised thermal room with a constant outdoor temperature of\n20 °C is to be heated from 20 to 25 °C. The temperature difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"><\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=T-20\\,\\mathrm{^\\circ C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis expressed in kelvin and the heating power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin kW. An idealised single-node thermal model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\dot x=-Gx+u+d.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkJ/K is the heat capacity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW/K the thermal conductance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nan additional heat input in kW. A negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents heat extraction. For constant inputs over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, the exact discrete model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=ax_k+b(u_k+d_k),\\qquad a=e^{-G\\Delta t/C},\\quad b=(1-a)/G.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The nominal coefficients are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.951229<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\approx0.951229<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.487706<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\approx0.487706<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK/kW. At steady state with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, the model requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0.5-d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW. In all cases, both controllers have the same limits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le u\\le2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW and perfect temperature measurements; they use the actual parameters\nin the first three cases. There are 120 intervals (60 min), with an\ninitial temperature of 20 °C and a setpoint of 25 °C. Four scenarios are\nconsidered:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case<\/th>\n<th>Plant<\/th>\n<th>Information available to both controllers<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal<\/td>\n<td>Nominal parameters,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=-0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW in intervals 40–79 (20–40 min)<\/td>\n<td>Current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_m=d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno preview of the end of the disturbance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown<\/td>\n<td>Same heat extraction<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mismatch<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW/K,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkJ/K,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Nominal model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The input constraint applies to heating power; no hard temperature\nlimit is claimed. The model is synthetic rather than identified from a\nreal building.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two controllers and objective function<\/h2>\n<p>PI with feedforward:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_k=5-x_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{raw}=0.1\\cdot5-d_m+0.45e_k+I_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">clip<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k=\\operatorname{clip}(u_{raw},0,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe integrator increases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.035<\/mn><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.035 e_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW per interval unless this would aggravate saturation (conditional\nintegration). The proportional gain has units of kW/K and the discrete\nintegral gain has units of kW/K per update. The initial integrator value\nis zero. Both controllers use the same nominal steady-state feedforward\nsignal.<\/p>\n<p>MPC uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_p=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na horizon of 360 s. It assumes the current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant at all future steps. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_i=a^i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{ij}=ba^{i-j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\le j\\le i\\le N_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=Fx_k+B(U+d_m\\mathbf1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cost is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/munderover><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(U)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N_p}w_i(x_{k+i|k}-5)^2+3\\sum_{i=0}^{N_p-1}(u_{k+i|k}-(0.5-d_m))^2,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_i=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{N_p}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNumerical calculations use temperature in K and power in kW; the weights\ncorrespond to normalisation by 1 K and 1 kW. The gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2[B^\\mathsf{T}W(X-r\\mathbf1)+3(U-u_{ff}\\mathbf1)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe strictly convex box-constrained problem is solved using L-BFGS-B.\nThe projected gradient check approximately verifies the KKT condition.\nThis is a simple QP solution for this example; the method should not be\ntreated as a generally real-time-capable solver.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible program code<\/h2>\n<p>Python 3, NumPy and SciPy are required. The simulation uses no random\nnumbers. <code>run<\/code> returns the state, control input and\ndisturbance sequences; the output computes the mean absolute temperature\nerror (after each step), maximum signed deviation above the setpoint,\nheating energy and final error. A negative maximum deviation indicates\nthat the setpoint was never reached. In the table below, overshoot is\nclipped to a minimum of zero. The energy is the integral of the ideal\nsupplied heating power over time, not a measurement of the electrical\nenergy consumed by the equipment. The original code uses the case labels\n<code>Bekannt<\/code> (Known) and <code>Unbekannt<\/code> (Unknown).<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> minimize<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> exp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>DT, H, N <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">30.<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>G, C <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">60.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>DT<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>G<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>C)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>G<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>F <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">**<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,H<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>B <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(i<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>j) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> j<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>i <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>               <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(H)] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(H)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>W <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.ones(H)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> W[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rho <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> mpc(x,r,d):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ff <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> G<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>d<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> F<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.full(H,d)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> obj(u):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ff<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.dot(W<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e,e)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rho<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.dot(v,v), <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(B.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>(W<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rho<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    res <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> minimize(obj,np.full(H,np.clip(ff,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)),jac<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                   bounds<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>)]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>H,method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;L-BFGS-B&#39;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                   options<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{<span class=\"st\">&#39;ftol&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"fl\">1e-13<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;gtol&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"fl\">1e-8<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;maxiter&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> res.success, res.message<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>res.x<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> grad<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>obj(u)[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.clip(u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>grad,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2e-4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> u[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> run(case,ctl):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    g,cap <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">.15<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">75.<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> case<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;Mismatch&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> (G,C)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ap <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>DT<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>cap)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> bp <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ap)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>g<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(N<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(N)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ds<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(N)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> integ<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(N):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> case <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&#39;Bekannt&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Unbekannt&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">40<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">80<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        ds[k]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> dm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> case<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;Bekannt&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[k]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> ctl<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;PI&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>G<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>dm<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.45<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>integ<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            u[k]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.clip(raw,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>raw<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> (raw<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> (raw<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                integ<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.035<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            u[k]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>mpc(x[k],r,dm)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x[k<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>ap<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[k]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>bp<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(u[k]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>((u<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ap<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>bp<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>ds)))<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> x,u,ds<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> case <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&#39;Nominal&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Bekannt&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Unbekannt&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Mismatch&#39;<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-50\"><a href=\"#cb1-50\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> ctl <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&#39;PI&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;MPC&#39;<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-51\"><a href=\"#cb1-51\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x,u,d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>run(case,ctl)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-52\"><a href=\"#cb1-52\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(case,ctl,<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>q<span class=\"sc\">:.5f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> q <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-53\"><a href=\"#cb1-53\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                np.mean(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)),np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(x)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-54\"><a href=\"#cb1-54\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(u)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>DT<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3600<\/span>,x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The checks require inputs within the box constraints, small discrete\nmodel residuals and successful optimisation with a small projected\ngradient residual. The model residual alone does not constitute\nindependent physical validation. An additional test compares the\nunconstrained solution in the unsaturated region with the linear system\nof equations derived from the quadratic cost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results and interpretation<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-13\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-13\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-18\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-18\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-18\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-18\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case<\/th>\n<th>Controller<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">MAE (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. overshoot (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Heating energy (kWh)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Final error (K)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal<\/td>\n<td>PI<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.18674<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.46398<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.57791<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00001<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal<\/td>\n<td>MPC<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.13544<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.56772<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.00000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known<\/td>\n<td>PI<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.18674<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.46398<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.81125<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00001<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known<\/td>\n<td>MPC<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.13544<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.80106<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.00000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown<\/td>\n<td>PI<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.49458<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.98242<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.81182<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.06512<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown<\/td>\n<td>MPC<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.58480<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.75612<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.00001<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mismatch<\/td>\n<td>PI<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.19812<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.20332<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.83250<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00001<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mismatch<\/td>\n<td>MPC<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.58583<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.75432<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.43917<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_093_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four sets of temperature and heating power trajectories for PI and MPC with an ideal model, measured and unknown thermal loads, and parameter mismatch.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Temperature and heater-power trajectories compare PI\nand MPC control under nominal and disturbed model conditions; all curves\narise from a simplified thermal model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In the undisturbed case, MPC is faster with these weights and avoids\novershoot. With a known load, both controllers compensate for the\nmeasured disturbance in the model through feedforward; the additional\nenergy in both cases is 0.7 kW × 20 min = 0.23333 kWh. With an unknown\ndisturbance, PI has a lower mean absolute error in this experiment but\nsubstantial overshoot after the load is removed. MPC without disturbance\nestimation cannot fully compensate for the temporary heat extraction at\nsteady state. With parameter mismatch, the simple MPC has a persistent\nfinal error of approximately −0.439 K despite continuous reoptimisation;\nPI adjusts the steady-state heating power through integration. The lower\nenergy demand of MPC in the mismatch case therefore does not\nautomatically indicate better efficiency, because the setpoint is not\nattained as closely.<\/p>\n<p>The two controllers were not retuned for each scenario. This is a\ncomparison of two transparently specified tunings, not a general\nranking. A different horizon, weight or PI gain may change the results.\nMPC with a disturbance estimator can also reduce steady-state error. A\nmeaningful real-world test requires independently determined model\nparameters, temperature and power data, sensor uncertainty, execution\ndeadlines and actual actuator limits. Real operation requires\nindependent temperature limiting. This example is not a construction or\noperating guide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Submission, extensions and review<\/h2>\n<p>Reproduce the eight cases and document software versions, input\nlimits, initial conditions, units and metrics. Change just one quantity\nat a time: the horizon (e.g. 6 instead of 12), the input weight (e.g. 6\ninstead of 3) or the modelled disturbance. Then add the estimator from\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L3&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L3<\/a>\nso that the steady-state input for the setpoint is adjusted. Compare\novershoot, error, energy and computational effort; also investigate\ncontrol authority reserves and an unattainable setpoint. Do not assess\nsuccess solely on the basis of a particularly favourable curve.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the steady-state heating power at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW/K and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.75 kW, not 0.5 kW.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK be maintained in the nominal model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=-1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkW? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> No; 2.3 kW would be required, whereas the\nmaximum is 2 kW. At steady state, at most 2 K is attainable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is comparing energy at different final temperatures problematic?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Different stored heat and different\naccumulated heat losses. Compare equivalent service quality or report\nerror and energy together.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which model information does MPC not use in the Known case?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The future time at which the load ends. The\ncurrent disturbance is held constant in its prediction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ethz.ch/en/studies/master/degree-programmes/engineering-sciences/electrical-engineering-and-information-technology.leftnav.html\">ETH\nZürich – Master Electrical Engineering and Information Technology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpecialisations: Systems and Control; curriculum profile comparison, not\nevidence of individual courses. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://sites.engineering.ucsb.edu/~jbraw/mpc/\">Rawlings/Mayne/Diehl\n– Model Predictive Control: Theory, Computation, and Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2nd\ned., 6th printing, §§1.3, 1.5, 2.5.1–2.5.4 (pp. 11, 46–49, 132–136); PDF\ncontents pages xv–xvi visually checked; no full claim review. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ee363.stanford.edu/lectures.html\">Stanford EE363 –\nLinear Dynamical Systems, Lecture Slides: LQR and Kalman Filter<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSpring 2026, lectures 16–18: finite and infinite horizon LQR; 22: Kalman\nfilter if needed; listing only, not evidence for the thermal project\nresults. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
